\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \{\uc0\u8232 "question_id": "Q03",\u8232 "marks": [\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 1,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "The proceedings could not be quashed only because the respondents had filed a civil suit with respect to the aforesaid documents.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly explains that a civil land dispute does not by itself prevent criminal proceedings for alleged cheating or forgery.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 2,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "several disputes of a civil nature may also contain the ingredients of criminal offences and if so, will have to be tried as criminal offences",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that criminal law can apply even when the underlying dispute is civil, if criminal ingredients are present.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 3,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "Merely because a civil claim is maintainable does not mean that the criminal complaint cannot be maintained.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly answers whether criminal proceedings can coexist with a civil dispute and specifically discusses cheating and forgery.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 4,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "merely because the nature of the dispute is primarily of a civil nature, the criminal prosecution cannot be quashed",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that the civil character of a dispute alone does not justify quashing criminal prosecution involving alleged forgery or fraud.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 5,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "any effort to settle civil disputes and claims, which do not involve any criminal offence, by applying pressure through criminal prosecution should be deprecated and discouraged.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly addresses misuse of criminal prosecution for civil disputes and then discusses allegations under Sections 420 and 467 IPC.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 6,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "the High Court must see whether a dispute which is essentially of a civil nature is given a cloak of criminal offence.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly provides the test for distinguishing genuine criminal allegations from a civil dispute dressed up as a criminal case.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 7,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "The Investigating Officer, however, upon investigation of the matter came to the conclusion that the dispute between the parties was a civil dispute.",\u8232 "reason": "It concerns a land dispute involving alleged forgery and cheating, but only reports the investigation's characterization rather than stating the governing rule.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 8,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "in the event the court finds that the dispute between the parties is civil in nature, it may not allow the criminal proceedings to go on.",\u8232 "reason": "It addresses whether civil disputes may bar criminal proceedings, but gives only a limited and qualified statement without the fuller test.",\u8232 "confidence": "medium"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 9,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "under certain situations where the act alleged would predominantly be a civil wrong, such an act does not constitute a criminal offence.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that predominantly civil conduct may not constitute a criminal offence, addressing the core issue in the question.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 10,\u8232 "mark": "Misleading",\u8232 "evidence": "It was further submitted that the allegations that the land in the question did not belong to the appellant could only be resolved by the Civil Court",\u8232 "reason": "This is an advocate's submission rather than the court's holding, so it could misleadingly suggest criminal proceedings are unavailable.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 11,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "execution of such document (purporting to convey some property of which he is not the owner) is not execution of a false document as defined under section 464 of the Code.",\u8232 "reason": "It specifically addresses forgery in a land-sale context, but does not generally answer whether criminal law can be used for a civil dispute.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 12,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "where the criminal complaints are filed in respect of property disputes of civil in nature only to harass the accused, and to pressurize him in the civil litigation pending",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that criminal proceedings arising only from a civil property dispute and used to harass or pressure may be quashed.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 13,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "where the criminal complaints are filed in respect of property disputes of civil in nature only to harass the accused, and to pressurize him in the civil litigation pending",\u8232 "reason": "It states the relevant quashing principle but the passage's main application finds a prima facie case of cheating, limiting its usefulness.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 14,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "The purely civil dispute, is sought to be given a colour of a criminal offence to wreak vengeance against the Appellant.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly concludes that a purely civil dispute presented as a criminal offence constitutes abuse of process.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 15,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "only because a civil remedy may be available to the complainant that itself cannot be a ground to quash a criminal proceeding.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly explains that a civil remedy does not automatically prevent criminal proceedings, while identifying the criminal-offence test for cheating.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 16,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "A matter which essentially involves dispute of a civil nature should not be allowed to be the subject matter of a criminal offence",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that an essentially civil dispute should not be converted into a criminal matter or used as a substitute for civil relief.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \}\u8232 ],\u8232 "good_answer_present": "Yes",\u8232 "best_passage_no": 2,\u8232 "caveats": ""\u8232 \}\
